#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae7e794d-b45c-5fa1-a6f8-0e6cf9bf6b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\ge -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377363} {:answer/id #uuid "1d68b7e9-6a85-5813-9310-df167cb3e035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\le -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377364} {:answer/id #uuid "48563982-89ee-5bb8-baec-cb82cd3e7083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}<-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377365} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc19fe0-3441-5697-a854-404e864c7432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377366} {:answer/id #uuid "161a53b4-89a7-59b6-909e-b03278987160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}>-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc19fe0-3441-5697-a854-404e864c7432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15b0709-d032-56bd-aac5-b65c83c62380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28ca8dae-bb66-5179-8a04-f47a13f5a86d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161596" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/2bb25857047d7a0200d4c58ee3756b0efa0f2d13aaca8664b1fe49414c18a461.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/88f35519cf1aa01bcc9e4fb91b24c2bc2a1a823867ec2670563b0b45f2643a0a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the parabola ${y}^{2}=-x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{y}^{2}=-x\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e172786e-070e-5772-87e4-cb0f67273db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377370} {:answer/id #uuid "adcf6414-1d3e-5c51-825c-39866b51f27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377371} {:answer/id #uuid "322b662e-5404-5635-aee4-3ab77363404f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377372} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed70b02-733f-5eae-8f02-c7c64dc01c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377373} {:answer/id #uuid "b648d80f-658d-5211-87f3-e39e80415e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adcf6414-1d3e-5c51-825c-39866b51f27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377371} :answer-field/id #uuid "85322f6b-516f-5402-8c45-cb902ded5ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84f3ca08-8934-5cbf-812e-630649f3de0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161601" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d796fa-a51e-50a8-8acc-fedc48733837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377377} {:answer/id #uuid "281888a4-8299-5625-95f0-9f5d0f785306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377378} {:answer/id #uuid "98dfc48c-f4a7-54d5-bd49-55b4cbac5dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377379} {:answer/id #uuid "b878bb07-2cb0-5f66-a06f-838dc9fcfcce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377380} {:answer/id #uuid "20a76a76-01af-5387-9d46-64dc59b35ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d796fa-a51e-50a8-8acc-fedc48733837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377377} :answer-field/id #uuid "c683ca29-1db4-5edb-92a8-944119592610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ee0de30-057d-5ea4-a8b3-df4f777834db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\n- $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "719376f3-5fc0-5913-b62f-f66b638d41f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377384} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9af5b4-9f9a-51a1-8849-24d0dd86db81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377385} {:answer/id #uuid "52a076ea-595b-5982-a2a6-084ca7bb30f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377386} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d7686d-5e63-5a0f-937e-5f0a359c9a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377387} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3713ff-d46f-5016-b769-f9378e17ac7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc3713ff-d46f-5016-b769-f9378e17ac7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377388} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ad199c-ed02-58fd-ae04-a2f3f15a51a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6614488c-3421-542d-a689-4aab19e43538" :question/math-academy-id "q-161721" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/6debac35386e085a0197cf9eb3f2da65cc719ace67163cad625f58c056fb8f67.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/c0b805c707981b9b6fcfa2ed387ad32a024fc845e6fd355a4b751ede4ad6a97e.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/69708aa698c38f8a29d3eb69083f893fd8fca183bd713387456624fc38b86fe9.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802abbf3-238f-50ff-9139-83436dcf5148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377391} {:answer/id #uuid "75b915bb-2128-5dd6-b2ea-23ac6e41721c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377392} {:answer/id #uuid "7425c5fa-26b7-50c1-8f9b-463fa1c37c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377393} {:answer/id #uuid "443ae0b2-776b-5a87-9232-50542b492303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377394} {:answer/id #uuid "f48ff385-bcd2-5c2a-bc2f-d61453678d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b915bb-2128-5dd6-b2ea-23ac6e41721c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377392} :answer-field/id #uuid "4917a447-5696-5f04-92f9-a8495961982b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d4d1dd-aca2-5029-99b9-a7becab58e6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71888" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0\\le y<2}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,,\\,0<y<2}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/f4ee8fbf9a7670a7d0e0b7488bccbf014f8db8fff0154c7db025f2b071aec566.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072304/assets/9fc733f6966b016c93b6ee6a305c5af5ee4fa432dad5d95e28061fd2a0d33efb.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<y<2}.\n$$"}]}
